1  Where it is
--------------
GameLib is in $.!Boot.Resources.!GameLib, with the other system
resources. Double-click !GameLib to see its help text, or open it
with SHIFT held down to see the modules (they are ordinary files of
type JSScript, so you can load any of them into !Edit and read it:
each starts with a few lines saying what it is for).

When the desktop starts it runs !GameLib's !Boot file, which sets
three system variables. Press F12 and type:

  *Show GameLib$*

to see them:

  GameLib$Dir      the library's directory
  GameLib$Path     where programs look for modules: a list of
                   directories, each ending in a '.', with commas
                   between (normally just <GameLib$Dir>.)
  GameLib$Version  the library's version, 1.00

A variable that is already set when the desktop starts is kept.

2  Importing a module
---------------------
In a JavaScript program (a file of type JSScript, run by double-
clicking it), import a module by its name after 'gamelib/':

  import { Loop } from 'gamelib/Loop';
  import { Surface, rgb } from 'gamelib/Surface';

This works wherever the program is. The name is looked for in each
directory of GameLib$Path in turn, and then in GameLib$Dir; the
first file found is used. 'GameLib/Loop' works too. A module name is
letters, digits, _ and -, so it cannot reach outside the library.

If a module is not found you are told where it was looked for:

  Can't find 'gamelib/Nope' (not in GameLib$Path: ...)

From an Obey file or the command line, the files are called
<GameLib$Dir>.Loop, or GameLib:Loop.

3  The modules
--------------
Maths      wrap and wrapDelta (positions on a space that repeats,
           such as a tunnel), clamp, lerp, and Rng, a random number
           generator you give a seed to, so a game can be played
           again exactly.

Loop       the main loop. The screen may redraw 60, 120 or 144
           times a second, but a game's world should move at one
           fixed rate. A Loop calls your tick() that many times a
           second (60 is usual) and your draw() once a frame. After
           a long pause it drops the lost ticks instead of racing
           to catch up.

Surface    a block of pixels, width by height, that you draw into
           in software, as on the Archimedes. rgb(r, g, b) makes a
           colour. Methods: clear, pixel, get, fillRect, hline,
           shade, blit (copy another surface, skipping one colour)
           and present (copy it to a canvas).

Raster     fills triangles and boxes a row at a time. Shapes uses
           it; a game seldom needs it.

Shapes     disc, pie (a disc with a mouth), arc, line, polygon and
           roundRect, drawn onto a Surface.

Font       the 8 by 8 pixel RISC OS system font, drawn onto a
           Surface: drawText (with scale, shadow, outline and
           alignment), drawLines and textWidth.

Display    where the picture appears: the whole desktop, or a
           window, with the surface scaled up to fit in whole
           pixels so it stays sharp. It looks after the window's
           close icon and losing the keyboard.

Keys       the keyboard as named actions. You say {left:
           ['ArrowLeft', 'KeyA']} and ask held('left'),
           pressed('left') or latest(['left', 'right']).

Pad        the first gamepad, as named presses (PadUp, PadA...).

Options    a game's settings, described by a list: each has a key,
           a label and its possible values. Checks saved settings
           and steps a setting up or down.

ScoreTable a high score table kept sorted: qualifies, insertScore
           and cleanTable (which checks a table read from a file).

Choices    where a game saves its files: <Choices$Write>.<name>,
           normally $.!Boot.Choices.<name>. Never fails: a game
           that cannot save carries on.

Audio      plays sounds through the browser. Sounds are made by
           the synthesiser below when first needed, played with
           play(), looped with loop(), and quietened with the
           desktop's speaker setting.

Synth      a small sound synthesiser: oscillators, noise and
           filters working on arrays of samples.
SynthEnv   the helpers that shape a sound over time: envelopes,
           sweeps, glides, steps and notes.
SynthFx    finishing touches: filter, echo, fadeOut, normalise,
           vidc8 (the gritty 8-bit sound of the old VIDC chip).

Everything except Display, Audio and Surface.present works without
a browser, so a game's rules can be tested from the command line
with Node.

4  A small game
---------------
This moves a yellow dot about with the arrow keys. Save it in !Edit
as a JSScript file (see the JavaScript books in Manuals) and double-
click it.

  import { wimp } from 'riscos';
  import { Loop } from 'gamelib/Loop';
  import { Surface, rgb } from 'gamelib/Surface';
  import { Display } from 'gamelib/Display';
  import { Keys } from 'gamelib/Keys';
  import { disc } from 'gamelib/Shapes';
  import { drawText } from 'gamelib/Font';

  export default function start(task, ctx) {
    const surface = new Surface(160, 120);
    const keys = new Keys({ left: ['ArrowLeft'], right: ['ArrowRight'],
      up: ['ArrowUp'], down: ['ArrowDown'] });
    const display = new Display({ task, wimp, os: ctx.os, title: 'Dot',
      width: 160, height: 120, background: '#000', onClose: quit });
    let x = 80, y = 60;
    const loop = new Loop({ hz: 60,
      tick() {
        x += keys.held('right') - keys.held('left');
        y += keys.held('down') - keys.held('up');
      },
      draw() {
        surface.clear(rgb(0, 0, 40));
        disc(surface, x, y, 6, rgb(255, 220, 0));
        drawText(surface, 'Arrows', 4, 4,
          { colour: rgb(255, 255, 255) });
        display.present(surface);
      } });
    const down = (e) => display.keysActive() && keys.keyDown(e)
      && e.preventDefault();
    const up = (e) => keys.keyUp(e);
    function quit() {
      loop.stop(); display.close(); task.quit();
      removeEventListener('keydown', down);
      removeEventListener('keyup', up);
    }
    addEventListener('keydown', down);
    addEventListener('keyup', up);
    display.open('window');
    loop.start();
  }

Display.open('full') takes the whole desktop instead. A real game
also stops when the window loses the keyboard (Display's onBlur;
call keys.release() there).

5  Using your own copy of a module
----------------------------------
To try a changed module without touching the library's, put the
module in a directory of your own, and put that directory first in
the path. After the desktop has started, press F12 and type:

  *Set GameLib$Path RAM::RamDisc0.$.MyLib.,<GameLib$Dir>.

Programs started after that find your Maths (say) before the
library's. A module's own imports are by relative name, so if you
change an inner module (SynthEnv, say) copy the modules that import
it as well. GameLib$Path is stored with <GameLib$Dir> filled in:
change GameLib$Dir later and you must set GameLib$Path again. To go
back, type *Unset GameLib$Path and run !GameLib's !Boot file.
